A||||13||Cooter||Help for torque steer?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||08-07-2000||01:54 AM||||I was tinkering with the GT tonight and noticed (after driving another car for a few weeks) that the Fiero tries to turn itself one way when accelerating and the other way when decelerating. The car is an 88GT, I have new polly on the arms and a recent 4 wheel alignment. The only thing not new is the rear struts. I think they are weak because the car really squats when you accelerate, but would they let it torque steer this bad?<P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||08-07-2000||02:14 PM||||it is imposible for a rear wheel drive car to "Torque steer". Torque steer happens when front drive cars over power thier drive wheels. when you turn the wheel and the engine over powers the inside wheel (single trac)  in a turn the wheel will pull the car in that direction makeing you have to fight the wheel. <BR> <BR>   I belive what you are feeling in your car is the engine shifting it's weight aroud. start the car and open the rear decklid and rev the engine I think you will see your engine move alot. look into poly dogbone bushings  and check all your motor mounts and trans mounts maybe even your cradle bolts & bushings. I don't think it would be the rear struts. If Your car is a 5-speed try the ultimate dogebone.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||08-07-2000||02:59 PM||||It sounds like the struts are bad anyway so replace them and see if it helps.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-07-2000||03:07 PM||||88? I thought the 88 had the cradle bolted solid. I'd look for cradle bushings or a control arm problem on an 84-87. 88's don't have cradle bushings.<P>Xtreme,<BR>there are a couple ways to get torque steer problems. What he's descibing is the other one. Usually happens in 84-87 Fieros because the motor shifts the cradle unevenly under hard braking or acceleration. It's caused by the rubber cradle mounts and the off center torque reaction strut.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||08-07-2000||04:05 PM||||The car also has new motor and tranny mounts with poly in the dogbone also. I checked all mounting bolts on the cradle and control arms and everything is tight. I will try new struts soon and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||08-07-2000||04:30 PM||||Cooter,<P>Is this on the 3.4? Does it only happen when your really getting on the throttle? If so, I'll have to dissagree with Xtreme Fiero's comment. Torque steer in the rear wheel can be caused when the bushings or lower arms deflect under load. Bad struts can compound this problem. Changes in the friction surface can also cause this (one wheel on dry pavement, the other on sand or gravel).<P>When this happens, the geometry changes (like turning the steering wheel on front drive cars). In Fieros, you'll feel the back end get a little unstable.<P>One recomendations I've read about is to "box" your lower control arms to add strength. Since the control arms are just thin stamped steel, this seems like it would be a good idea. Especially if your making a lot of power.<P>My $0.02 worth,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||08-07-2000||04:35 PM||||I re-read you note and, to be more specific, you may want to check and see if one of the bushing went bad. Try setting the emergency brake and jacking the rear of the car (rest on jackstands for safety). Take a pry bar and, using the wheel studs, pry the rotor back and forth, Up and down. See if you get more movement on one wheel. If so, look there first.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000007||Cooter||08-07-2000||08:20 PM||||It is in the 3.4 car and it is most noticable when you accelerate hard then let off the throttle quickly, but it does it even during normal driving. I will put it on the lift again and check for a bad bushing.<P>Thanks-<P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Xtreme Fiero's||08-07-2000||09:15 PM||||how bout "Bump Steer" you say the car <BR>squats when you hit the gass, that <BR>may cause the rear wheels to toe out <BR>\/ then you let off the gas and the <BR>car may toe in /\. but I didn't think <BR>the 88' GT's & formula's did that and <BR>only the 84-87's did that. Jus a <BR>thought.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>OGRE,<BR> Can you please explain how a rear <BR>wheel drive can torque steer? I have <BR>never heard of this ever hapening. <BR>Thanks, ||||reg||5
Z||000009||Raydar||08-07-2000||10:49 PM||||Cooter... Whatever you want to call it, my 88 coupe does it. On the gas, the front end lifts, pulls to the left. Off the gas, the front drops, pulls to the right. The Formula does it just a bit, but not nearly as much.<BR>I have found that tire pressure seems to make a big difference. Check to see if your right rear isn't low. (I suppose that weak struts could cause the same symptom. Especially if the right one was weaker.)<BR>Just my $.02.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||08-08-2000||03:46 PM||||If the struts are squating bad they can change toe and camber. That certainly could acount for an 88 doing this.<P>Xtreame,<P>We've generally called it torque steer, call it whatever, It's caused when engine load shifts right side of the cradle more than the left effectivly rotating the whole rear like a set of waggon wheels. At the least it's annoying. It can be very dangerous.<P>This wouldn't happen except that Fiero's Torque strut is mounted as far right as posible and is connected to the body.<P>Manual tranny cars do it much worse than automatics. In an auto tranny the TC absorbs allot of the suden changes. With a bigger motor the auto will not likely be able to absorb it as well.<P>It can only happen with a rubber mounted cradle that has control arms on it. Allot of front cradles don't have control arms on them or they are solid mounted. Many FWD's don't even have cradles. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Patrick||08-08-2000||06:57 PM||||<BR>My '87 with a 5-speed has bad torque steer. I like to think that this is the result of the enormous power being produced by the mighty 2.5L duke. Yes, that's what I <I>like</I> to think. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> In reality it's probably a mushy cradle bushing that's responsible. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000012||squid||08-08-2000||08:48 PM||||"Torque steer" is due mostly to differences in cv shaft lengths causing one wheel to recieve more power than another causing a difference in toe, whether its in the front or the rear of the car. Manual cars have a much greater difference in cv shaft lengths than automatic cars. On a manual car the right shaft is at least twice as long as the left.I dont know that you can do anything about it except urethane lower control arm bushings and new tierods to minimize toe changes. I think Ryane makes a toe change eliminating lower control arm setup that would do it but its pretty expensive.  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||FIEROCIOUS||08-09-2000||12:49 AM||||Hmmm, no torque steer in a rear wheel drive?<BR>Someones never driven a car with posi traction! <BR>Torque steer for everyones info is the inertia of the engine causeing a gyroscopic effect upon the car chasis.<BR>On fiero's this is more easily done than most rear wheel front engine cars due to the mid-engine layout. The front engine rear drive must tranfer the inertia to the driveline then to the drive axles. On a posi-traction equiped cars the shifting is due more to weight being apllied unevenly to the drive wheels (body flex and engine torque.)<P>Because it's been described to do it during acceleration AND deceleration this is not torque steer and is caused most likely by worn suspention components.<P>Hope this helps.   ||||reg||1
